<interrogationes unitas="pecunia">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I split a bill so the cents add up?
    <responsum symbolum="pecunia_dividere pecunia_partiri">Use
      pecunia_dividere(total, n, PECUNIA_GRANUM_UNITAS, piscina, parts)
      for equal shares, or pecunia_partiri with an array of ratios for
      weighted ones. Both use largest remainder: the parts always sum to
      the total exactly (USD 10.00 / 3 = 3.34, 3.33, 3.33), ties go to
      the earlier part, and a zero ratio never gets a leftover cent.
      With PECUNIA_GRANUM_UNITAS the total must already be payable,
      otherwise PECUNIA_VITIUM_NON_SOLVIBILIS.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I store an amount in an event or a file?
    <responsum symbolum="pecunia_ad_textum pecunia_ex_textu">Write it with
      pecunia_ad_textum ("USD -12.3400": upper-case code, one space,
      always four decimals) and read it back with pecunia_ex_textu,
      which accepts ONLY that exact form. Every value has one spelling,
      so equal text means equal value and diffs stay clean. Never store
      the display form from pecunia_formare.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why is an amount someone typed refused?
    <responsum symbolum="pecunia_ex_ingressu pecunia_vitium_titulus">pecunia_ex_ingressu
      is lenient only by fixed rules and returns a named PecuniaVitium -
      pass it to pecunia_vitium_titulus for a message. Group separators
      must sit in real positions ("1,234.56" yes, "12,34" no - that
      could mean 1234 or 12.34, and the library refuses rather than
      guesses), only one sign is allowed, at most four decimals, and a
      currency code in the text must match the expected currency.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I round an amount to cents (or to the currency's minor unit)?
    <responsum symbolum="pecunia_ad_unitatem pecunia_solvibilis">pecunia_ad_unitatem(amount,
      modus, &amp;result) rounds to the minor unit from the ISO table
      (USD 3.4590 -> 3.46 with PECUNIA_PAR; JPY to whole yen). The mode
      is always explicit: PECUNIA_PAR (banker's), PECUNIA_DIMIDIUM_SURSUM
      (half away from zero), PECUNIA_VERSUS_NIHIL, PECUNIA_SOLUM,
      PECUNIA_TECTUM. pecunia_solvibilis tells you whether an amount is
      already a whole number of minor units.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I convert an amount to another currency?
    <responsum symbolum="pecunia_convertere">Pass the rate as an exact
      Fractio (units of the target per one unit of the source, greater
      than zero) to pecunia_convertere with a rounding mode. The product
      is exact and rounded once to four decimals; call
      pecunia_ad_unitatem afterwards for a payable amount. pecunia never
      fetches rates and never mixes currencies silently - adding USD to
      EUR is PECUNIA_VITIUM_MONETAE_DIVERSAE.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I read and write amounts in Lunch Money's API format?
    <responsum symbolum="pecunia_ex_numero pecunia_numerus">Lunch Money
      sends amounts as strings matching ^-?\d+(\.\d{1,4})?$ in any
      currency. pecunia_ex_numero(text, moneta, &amp;amount) reads exactly
      that (leading zeros allowed, five decimals refused as
      PECUNIA_VITIUM_DECIMALES); pecunia_numerus writes "-12.3400". The
      currency comes separately, from pecunia_moneta_ex_codice, which
      accepts Lunch Money's lower-case codes.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I use crypto amounts such as BTC or ETH?
    <responsum symbolum="pecunia_moneta_ex_codice PECUNIA_SCALA">No. Every
      amount is an s64 count of ten-thousandths (PECUNIA_SCALA), which
      covers all ISO currencies but not 18-decimal crypto units, so
      pecunia_moneta_ex_codice refuses BTC and ETH as
      PECUNIA_VITIUM_MONETA_IGNOTA. Withdrawn currencies (BGN, HRK...)
      and GGP/IMP/JEP are in the table, marked by status.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
